original_answer_raw:
We do some simplification, taking advantage of the fact that $n! = n\cdot (n-1)!$: \begin{align*}
\frac{9!\cdot 5!\cdot 2!}{8!\cdot 6!} &= \frac{9\cdot 8! \cdot 5! \cdot 2}{8!\cdot 6\cdot 5!}\\
&= \frac{9\cdot 2}{6}\\
&= \boxed{3}.
\end{align*}

llama_partial_cot_response:
To continue the reasoning:

We can now substitute the expanded factorials into the original expression:
\begin{align*}
(9! \cdot 5! \cdot 2!) / (8! \cdot 6!) &= \frac{(9\times8\times7\times6\times5\times4\times3\times2\times1) \cdot (5\times4\times3\times2\times1) \cdot (2\times1)}{(8\times7\times6\times5\times4\times3\times2\times1) \cdot (6\times5\times4\times3\times2\times1)}
\end{align*}

Next, we can cancel out common factors between the numerator and denominator:
\begin{align*}
(9! \cdot 5! \cdot 2!) / (8! \cdot 6!) &= \frac{(9\times\cancel{8}\times7\times\cancel{6}\times5\times4\times3\times\cancel{2}\times1) \cdot (\cancel{5}\times4\times3\times\cancel{2}\times1) \cdot (\cancel{2}\times1)}{(\cancel{8}\times7\times\cancel{6}\times5\times4\times3\times\cancel{2}\times1) \cdot (\cancel{6}\times\cancel{5}\times4\times3\times\cancel{2}\times1)}
\end{align*}

After cancelling out the common factors, we are left with:
\begin{align*}
(9! \cdot 5! \cdot 2!) / (8! \cdot 6!) &= \frac{(9\times7) \cdot 4}{7 \cdot 4} \\
&= \frac{9\times7}{7} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{9\cdot7}{7}} \\
&= \boxed{9}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the value of $(9! \cdot 5! \cdot 2!) / (8! \cdot 6!)$ is $\boxed{9}$.

original_answer_raw:
Let $s$ be the side length of the cube.  Then the only possible distances of between two vertices of the cube are $s,$ $s \sqrt{2},$ and $s \sqrt{3}.$

[asy]
import graph;

unitsize(3 cm);

draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(1.3,0.3));
draw((1,1)--(1.3,1.3));
draw((0,1)--(0.3,1.3));
draw((1.3,0.3)--(1.3,1.3)--(0.3,1.3));
draw((0,0)--(0.3,0.3),dashed);
draw((0.3,0.3)--(1.3,0.3),dashed);
draw((0.3,0.3)--(0.3,1.3),dashed);
draw((1.3,1.3)--(0,1));
draw((0,1)--(1.3,0.3),dashed);

label("$s$", ((1.3,1.3) + (1.3,0.3))/2, E, red);
label("$s \sqrt{2}$", ((1.3,1.3) + (0,1))/2, NW, red);
label("$s \sqrt{3}$", ((0,1) + (1.3,0.3))/2, SW, red);
[/asy]

Since $AB = 2 \sqrt{6},$ $BC = 4 \sqrt{3},$ and $AC = 6 \sqrt{2},$ and
\[2 \sqrt{6} < 4 \sqrt{3} < 6 \sqrt{2},\]they must be equal to $s,$ $s \sqrt{2},$ and $s \sqrt{3},$ respectively.  Furthermore, the only lengths of $s \sqrt{3}$ are space diagonals of the cube.

This means that $\overline{AC}$ is a space diagonal of the cube, so the center of the cube is the midpoint of $\overline{AC},$ which is $\boxed{(3,2,4)}.$

